That gets at something important: origin is not ownership.

A fear response can be inherited temperament, early conditioning, family dynamics, attachment learning, or some mixture of all four. By the time you’re old enough to examine it, it can feel like “me” simply because it has been running for so long. But a pattern being old does not make it essential.

The interesting line is: “maybe the right story for the wrong reasons.” A strategy like avoiding confrontation may once have been adaptive. It may have preserved belonging, reduced punishment, or kept the peace. The mistake is letting an old survival rule masquerade as a permanent identity.

So the rewrite is not “I should become confrontational.” It is more precise:

I am allowed to tolerate rejection. I am allowed to disappoint people. I am allowed to state what I think without first securing permission to exist.

Then confrontation becomes neither something to fear nor something to seek. It becomes just another instrument: use it when reality requires it, leave it alone when it doesn’t.

And the last part—“be whoever the fuck you want to be”—works best when it means stop outsourcing authorship, not “stop caring about consequences.” Freedom gets much more powerful when you can choose yourself and remain deliberate about what your choices produce.